  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nBy Tuesday morning, a broad 500 MB high pressure ridge will be \nlocated over most of the central and eastern U.S. However, a \nweak shortwave will be moving off the Rockies and over the High \nPlains. This feature will pass slowly near Iowa on Tuesday into \nWednesday, bringing some mid/high level clouds across the region\nduring that time. A few model solutions even attempt to spit \nout light rainfall, however, forecast soundings indicate \nsubstantial dry air in the lower/near-surface levels with flow \nout of the Great Lakes ridge, which should preclude any \nmeaningful chances of precipitation. Meanwhile, despite some \nincrease in cloud cover, airmass modification should allow for \ncontinuation of a slow warming trend with temperatures peaking \nin the lower 70s or so on Wednesday afternoon.\n\nFrom late Wednesday into Friday a more pronounced 500 MB ridge \nwill build over the central U.S., including Iowa, supporting \ncontinued dry weather and slow warming. Highs in the 70s are \nforecast both Thursday and Friday. Eventually, by the end of the\nweek, a deep 500 MB trough will move into the western U.S. \nRockies and bring a return of more changeable weather and some \nrain chances by next weekend. Any details of timing/location of \nrain chances and other sensible weather fields are still in flux\nat this range, but will steadily come into better focus as this\nweek progresses.",
